lessons.writtenInstructions:
## Step 1 — Provision the catalog and its three schemas in Terraform

```hcl
# main.tf
resource "databricks_catalog" "harborview" {
  name    = "harborview"
  comment = "Harborview Health Partners -- governed claims lakehouse"

  properties = {
    purpose = "claims-analytics"
  }
}

resource "databricks_schema" "bronze" {
  catalog_name = databricks_catalog.harborview.name
  name         = "bronze"
  comment      = "Raw, append-only claims data landed by Auto Loader."
}

resource "databricks_schema" "silver" {
  catalog_name = databricks_catalog.harborview.name
  name         = "silver"
  comment      = "Validated, deduplicated claims -- Lakeflow Declarative Pipeline."
}

resource "databricks_schema" "gold" {
  catalog_name = databricks_catalog.harborview.name
  name         = "gold"
  comment      = "Business-facing aggregates and the governed patient-level table."
}
```

## Step 2 — Provision the three Unity Catalog groups

```hcl
resource "databricks_group" "compliance_team" {
  display_name = "compliance_team"
}

resource "databricks_group" "clinical_quality" {
  display_name = "clinical_quality"
}

resource "databricks_group" "billing_analysts" {
  display_name = "billing_analysts"
}
```

## Step 3 — Grant catalog- and schema-level usage so the groups can actually reach the data later

```hcl
resource "databricks_grants" "harborview_catalog" {
  catalog = databricks_catalog.harborview.name

  grant {
    principal  = databricks_group.compliance_team.display_name
    privileges = ["USE_CATALOG"]
  }
  grant {
    principal  = databricks_group.clinical_quality.display_name
    privileges = ["USE_CATALOG"]
  }
  grant {
    principal  = databricks_group.billing_analysts.display_name
    privileges = ["USE_CATALOG"]
  }
}

resource "databricks_grants" "gold_schema" {
  schema = "${databricks_catalog.harborview.name}.${databricks_schema.gold.name}"

  grant {
    principal  = databricks_group.compliance_team.display_name
    privileges = ["USE_SCHEMA", "SELECT"]
  }
  grant {
    principal  = databricks_group.clinical_quality.display_name
    privileges = ["USE_SCHEMA", "SELECT"]
  }
  grant {
    principal  = databricks_group.billing_analysts.display_name
    privileges = ["USE_SCHEMA", "SELECT"]
  }
}
```

Granting `SELECT` on the gold schema now is deliberately safe to do before a single row filter exists, because `claims_detail_gold` itself doesn't exist yet either -- the row filter and column masks designed in Lesson 6 get attached to that table the moment it's built, before any of these three groups could query a real row.

## Step 4 — Apply, and confirm with the equivalent SQL

```bash
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply
```

```sql
-- What Terraform just created, confirmed by hand
SHOW CATALOGS LIKE 'harborview';
SHOW SCHEMAS IN harborview;
-- bronze
-- gold
-- silver

SHOW GROUPS LIKE '%compliance_team%';
SHOW GROUPS LIKE '%clinical_quality%';
SHOW GROUPS LIKE '%billing_analysts%';
```

## Step 5 — Add the data classification tag framework now, before any PII column exists to apply it to

```sql
-- Confirms the pii_category tag is usable in this catalog; the tag
-- itself gets applied to patient_name/patient_ssn once
-- claims_detail_gold is built, per the Lesson 6 design
ALTER CATALOG harborview SET TAGS ('classification_framework' = 'phi-v1');
```

## Step 6 — Write down exactly what this lesson did NOT do

```
NOT YET BUILT:
  - harborview.bronze.claims_raw (no Auto Loader stream running yet)
  - harborview.silver.claims_clean (no Lakeflow pipeline yet)
  - claims_summary_gold / claims_detail_gold (no tables yet)
  - The clinic_access_lookup lookup table, the row filter function, or
    either column mask function designed in Lesson 6
  - Actual human membership inside the three groups -- that's an
    account-console or SCIM step for Harborview's IT admin, scoped
    deliberately outside this build

This lesson built the container and the identities everything else
attaches to. Every one of the items above gets built directly on top
of what's provisioned here, in the lessons that follow.
```

## Step 1 — Name the three tasks and the dependency chain before opening the Workflows UI

The nightly job has exactly one valid order: Bronze has to land before Silver can clean it, and Silver has to finish before Gold can aggregate it.

```text
bronze_ingest_claims_raw  -->  silver_pipeline_update  -->  gold_refresh
```

Task 1 runs the Auto Loader ingestion notebook from Lesson 9. Task 2 triggers an update of the `claims_clean` Lakeflow Declarative Pipeline from Lesson 10. Task 3 runs the notebook that recomputes `claims_summary_gold` and `claims_detail_gold` from Lesson 11.

## Step 2 — Write the job as a JSON spec, not just clicks in the UI

```json
{
  "name": "harborview_claims_nightly",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "task_key": "bronze_ingest_claims_raw",
      "notebook_task": {
        "notebook_path": "/Repos/harborview/ingestion/bronze_claims_autoloader"
      },
      "new_cluster": {
        "spark_version": "15.4.x-scala2.12",
        "node_type_id": "Standard_DS3_v2",
        "num_workers": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "task_key": "silver_pipeline_update",
      "depends_on": [{ "task_key": "bronze_ingest_claims_raw" }],
      "pipeline_task": { "pipeline_id": "a1b2c3d4-claims-clean" }
    },
    {
      "task_key": "gold_refresh",
      "depends_on": [{ "task_key": "silver_pipeline_update" }],
      "notebook_task": {
        "notebook_path": "/Repos/harborview/gold/refresh_claims_summary_and_detail"
      },
      "existing_cluster_id": "0821-shared-gold"
    }
  ],
  "schedule": {
    "quartz_cron_expression": "0 0 2 * * ?",
    "timezone_id": "America/New_York",
    "pause_status": "UNPAUSED"
  },
  "email_notifications": {
    "on_failure": ["data-platform@harborviewhealth.com"]
  },
  "max_concurrent_runs": 1
}
```

A JSON job definition, checked into the same repo as the pipeline code, means the schedule and dependency chain are reviewable and version-controlled the same way the SQL and Python in Lessons 9 through 11 already are.

## Step 3 — Pick 2 AM for a real operational reason, not an arbitrary one

Harborview's 15 clinics finish submitting the day's claims batches by 11 PM local time, and the clinics' own billing export — the independent number Lesson 15 eventually compares against — runs at 1 AM. Scheduling the job for 2 AM guarantees every clinic's claims are already sitting in `claims_raw` before Bronze ingestion starts, and that the billing export has already run before anyone could compare the two numbers mid-pipeline.

## Step 4 — Make max_concurrent_runs: 1 deliberate, not a default left alone

If last night's run is still finishing — a slow Auto Loader backlog, a long Lakeflow pipeline update — a second run must never start on top of it. Two overlapping runs against the same `claims_clean` table could race each other and leave Gold aggregating a half-updated Silver layer. `max_concurrent_runs: 1` makes that impossible by construction.

## Step 5 — Wire failure alerting to the whole job, not just the last task

`email_notifications.on_failure` fires if any of the three tasks fails — Bronze, Silver, or Gold — not only if the final `gold_refresh` task fails. A Bronze failure that goes unnoticed because only the last task alerts is exactly how a pipeline quietly runs on stale data for days before anyone notices a report looks wrong.

## Step 6 — Prove the dependency chain actually blocks a bad run

Trigger the job manually, then deliberately break the Bronze notebook (point it at a nonexistent file path) and re-run. Confirm `silver_pipeline_update` and `gold_refresh` both show as **Skipped**, not **Succeeded** against stale data, and confirm the failure email arrives. Revert the break and re-run once more to confirm a clean, fully green pass before moving on.
